            <h3>Description</h3>

    <p><img src="/images/puzzles/1.1-btc-phemex-puzzle.png" class="w-100"
            alt="1.1 BTC Puzzle by Phemex"></p>

    <p>2.1 BTC are hidden in this picture, can you find the solution? If you can’t, tag the smartest person you know
        on Twitter, and you will receive up to $100 if they solve it!</p>

    <p>Everyone in the RT chain that led the winner to the puzzle will also receive a $100 trading bonus.
        Once a participant has found the correct private key in the image, they will be able to transfer 1.1 BTC to
        the public key address of their own wallet. A further 1 BTC will be deposited to their Phemex account once
        they prove they control the private key.</p>

    <p>The public key is: 1h8BNZkhsPiu6EKazP19WkGxDw3jHf9aT.</p>

    <h3>Hints</h3>

    <ol>
        <li>The first 21-digit prime found in consecutive digits of e is: 957496696762772407663</li>
        <li>The private key you derive from Satoshi’s portrait is a big integer, not Wallet Import Format (WIF)</li>
        <li>The filename of the picture is irrelevant</li>
        <li>The next step involves converting some words from the portrait, without I/O, into a 27-digit number</li>
        <li>Go back to step 4) again if you can’t figure it out</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Last official hint:</p>
    <p>After meeting the Phemex team, the Goddess Pheme repeated the words “little is big”, twice within
        three days.</p>

    <h3>Solution</h3>

    <p>The first 21-digit prime found in consecutive digits of e is: 957496696762772407663.</p>
    <p>27-digit number: 237871847045914904726285415 (first hint came out on Jan. 23, two times within three days,
        also refer to the first two digits:23), b58decode(‘SatoshiNakamoto’), convert the bytes to integer using
        little endian, which is bigger than that of big endian, charset(default):
        ’123456789ABCDEFGHJKLMNPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijkmnopqrstuvwxyz’.</p>
    <p>Last number: 554405551875, b58decode(‘Phemex’) convert the bytes to integer using little endian,which is also
        bigger than that of big endian, charset(xrp):
        ’rpshnaf39wBUDNEGHJKLM4PQRST7VWXYZ2bcdeCg65jkm8oFqi1tuvAxyz’.</p>
    <p>This is the final private key in decimal: 126272244427365764086102017718794198001099243823071433146875 =
        957496696762772407663*237871847045914904726285415 * 554405551875,
        0x141dc7bec50472bb381be8e18f6d6b397773d71fc5d91d41fb in hexadecimal.</p>

